<figure class="two-clocks" aria-labelledby="two-clocks-title"><figcaption id="two-clocks-title">Figure 1 · The schema job and the application have separate completion points</figcaption><div class="paper-surface clocks"><div class="lane"><strong>Database work</strong><div class="route"><span>Publish compatible definitions</span><b aria-hidden="true">→</b><span>Fill and validate stored data</span><b aria-hidden="true">→</b><span>Job succeeded</span></div></div><div class="lane"><strong>Application contract</strong><div class="route"><span>Old explicit-column callers continue</span><b aria-hidden="true">→</b><span>Check equivalent prices and receipts</span><b aria-hidden="true">→</b><span>Enable new readers</span></div></div><p class="boundary">The upper lane cannot prove the lower lane's acceptance. Neither lane proves that old writers have retired.</p><div class="authority"><span><strong>Decimal price</strong><br/>Writable authority</span><b aria-hidden="true">→ ×100 →</b><span><strong>Stored hundredths</strong><br/>Derived; cannot be assigned independently</span></div></div><p class="caption">Conceptual dependencies, not a trace of CockroachDB's internal schema states. In this chosen computed-column design the decimal remains authoritative throughout; the drawing does not promise a writable integer-only interface.</p></figure>
